# T-11657 — the two HONEST NON-PASS TERMINALS a deferred probe can reach.
#
# Before this, the ONLY exit from `deferred` was `pass`, so a criterion whose proof can NEVER arrive
# (evidence living in a consumer repo; a before/after whose baseline was never recorded; a measure
# unobservable in principle — a SILENT fallback leaves no trace by definition) and a criterion whose
# reading ARRIVED and came back NEGATIVE both had no exit at all. Measured cost, stated ONLY to the
# precision that was actually measured: of 168 criteria in the deferred-probe debt view, 19 are
# CONSUMER-OWNED — their evidence lives in another repository, which the scope boundary already
# forbids being an acceptance-probe target, so they are MIS-AUTHORED rather than pending and no
# amount of work moves them. Six of the 19 are individually identified (the 2026-06-15 batch:
# T-0835 AC3, T-0840 AC3, T-0841 AC3, T-0849 AC3, T-0860, T-0864 AC3 — followup fu_3905b82ae054).
# The card's original bullet ALSO claimed 4 further criteria unreachable for a GRAMMAR reason; that
# half is FALSE and the corpus had already established it (T-11655, wont-do «premise false»:
# `_parse_settle_pairs` has split on the LAST colon since T-11531, so a colon-bearing probe key is
# addressable by passing the full recorded key verbatim — demonstrated on T-0425, commit de0cbcbd1).
# It is recorded here as CORRECTED rather than deleted, so a reader who meets the claim elsewhere
# knows it was retracted and does not re-derive the settled grammar question (fu_73ae05baab19). The
# derived «one in seven» proportion rested on that false half and is therefore NOT quoted — re-measure
# it before using a figure. NONE of this changes the case for the two terminals: it changes only the
# SIZE of the floor they make honest.
# The falsified arm is likewise not hypothetical: five instances (T-10845, T-10850, T-10875,
# T-11471 AC4, and the oldest — T-0419 AC3, falsified by that card's OWN verification run and
# recorded at the time, events.jsonl#ts=2026-06-06T10:00:08Z).
#
# TWO VALUES, NOT ONE, and NOT one value plus a reason that happens to say which (owner ruling
# 2026-08-27, events.jsonl#ts=2026-08-27T03:10:47Z): the two states differ in what a reader should
# CONCLUDE about the card — `unreachable` says a proof can never arrive (the remedy is an authoring
# fix, T-11656 / SPEC-0060), `falsified` says one arrived and was negative (the remedy is a real
# finding someone may need to act on) — so the distinction belongs in the STORED VALUE, where the
# debt view can count each separately and a reader can tell them apart on read-back.
#
# They live HERE, in the ONE recorded-probe-value vocabulary, because `bin/lib/views.py` states the
# design intent outright: "the discharge is keyed on the value the settle itself writes — never on a
# parallel marker that could drift from it". A side store would be exactly that marker.
#
# CLOSE-GATE SAFETY — this widens the SETTLE, never the close. `probe_passed` is
# `all(v == "pass" for v in non_deferred.values())`, so a card that tried to CLOSE carrying a
# terminal is refused exactly as `fail` is refused today. Nothing here changes what may be DEFERRED
# at closure either.
PROBE_RESULT_VOCAB = ("pass", "fail", "deferred", "unreachable", "falsified")


# T-12237 — THE ONE PLACE THE `probe_passed` AGGREGATE IS DERIVED after a late-linkage settlement.
# Every settlement leg calls this, so they cannot drift apart from each other or from the close write.
#
# THE DEFECT. `task close` computed `probe_passed` over the NON-DEFERRED probes only, so a card with
# some passing and some DEFERRED probes wrote `probe_passed: true` — claiming a proof it does not
# have. T-12223 closed exactly that way (AC1/AC3 deferred, `probe_passed: true`) and its post-close
# re-audit RED-ed on the contradiction, which the consult then held on. It IS a real contradiction,
# and the verb wrote it.
#
# THE FIX EXTENDS AN EXISTING VOCABULARY RATHER THAN ADDING ONE (CHARTER §P1 F1/F2): `deferred` is
# already the probe-result token for precisely this state (`probes: {AC2: deferred}`), so the
# AGGREGATE gains the same third value. No new field, no new event type, no new flag; `probe_passed`
# is already in the `task_closed` payload, so no NEW journal payload key is introduced either.
def _recompute_probe_passed(task: dict, current):
    """The `probe_passed` aggregate for THIS card's `probes:`. THREE branches, in this order:

      1. ANY probe still `deferred` -> `"deferred"`. The debt is undischarged, so the card must not
         claim a pass it has not got.
      2. No `deferred` left AND every probe is `pass` -> `True`. This is the ONLY branch that may
         assert a pass, and it asserts one only when every criterion actually passed.
      3. No `deferred` left but SOME probe carries a non-pass result (`fail`, or the T-11657
         terminals `unreachable` / `falsified`) -> return `current` UNCHANGED, so the caller can WARN
         naming the non-pass probe(s). FAIL-CLOSED: a settlement can NEVER rewrite a non-pass state
         to `true`.

    Why branch 3 returns the CURRENT value rather than True/False: this card governs the DEFERRED
    aggregate, and T-11657 deliberately decided what a post-close TERMINAL does to `probe_passed`
    («this widens the SETTLE, never the close»). Re-deciding that here would be a scope change on a
    settled question; refusing to overwrite it is the fail-closed reading.

    Returns `(value, non_pass_keys)`. Pure f(card); no I/O."""
    probes = task.get("probes")
    if not isinstance(probes, dict) or not probes:
        return current, []
    vals = {str(k): str(v).strip().lower() for k, v in probes.items()}
    if any(v == "deferred" for v in vals.values()):
        return "deferred", []
    non_pass = sorted(k for k, v in vals.items() if v != "pass")
    if non_pass:
        return current, non_pass
    return True, []
