# --- T-11742 (X-1157): the `task update --from-stdin` key classification --------------------------
# The ingest above reads the WHOLE stdin mapping and folds back exactly PROSE_BEARING_UPDATE_FIELDS.
# Every OTHER top-level key used to be discarded with no output and exit 0 — the same silence
# T-10738 removed from `task file --from-stdin` (the four-bucket classification at the top of this
# module), on the sibling verb.
#
# WHY IT IS WORSE HERE, which is why <project> ranked the report high: `task file` LOSES work.
# `task update` loses work AND WRITES A NOTE ASSERTING IT DID NOT. `note` and the dropped field
# arrive in the SAME mapping, so the recognised half lands while the unrecognised half vanishes:
# measured 2026-08-28, a mapping carrying `note: corrected AC1 per owner` + a corrected `acceptance`
# exited 0, printed "amendment note recorded (1 total)", and left the card's acceptance reading its
# ORIGINAL text. The card then testifies to an edit absent from its own acceptance, and a later
# reader closing against that note closes against an uncorrected criterion — a manufactured false
# evidence on a governed artifact, which a reader cannot detect from the card alone.
#
# TWO buckets, not T-10738's four, because this verb's input surface is different: `task update` is
# NOT a card rebuilder. It has no PASSTHROUGH bucket (passthrough is exactly "the set of fields
# `task update` may write", which is a different question and a different card) and no VERB-OWNED
# bucket (it authors no lifecycle field from stdin). So a non-ingested key is either a real input of
# this verb that rides ARGV, or nothing at all.
#
# BOTH memberships are DERIVED, never hand-listed (CHARTER §P5; the anti-pattern in
# lessons/a-widening-log-means-invert-the-whitelist.md is a kernel-owned whitelist that can only ever
# be behind): INGESTED is PROSE_BEARING_UPDATE_FIELDS itself — the constant that already drives the
# fold-back loop, so the classifier and the ingest can never disagree — and ARGV-ONLY is the argparse
# Namespace's own dests, which IS the set this verb accepts on argv. A flag added to `task update`
# tomorrow is classified correctly with no change here.
#
# REFUSAL, not a WARN, and BEFORE any write: it fires above `_load_task_for_transition`, so no note
# can be written for an edit that did not land. That posture is this verb's OWN, taken from the
# adjacent --old/--new guard whose comment states the reason exactly — a silently-ignored input
# "would be worse than a drop, because the caller would read the resulting exit 0 as 'the edit
# applied'".
# T-11873: the classifier ITSELF now lives at `textutil.classify_stdin_keys`, beside the ingest
# that drives it, and the refusal is raised there — so every `--from-stdin` caller gets what
# only this verb had. `task update` keeps the two DERIVATIONS below and nothing else.


# `task pause`: --reason is a CLOSED enum (PAUSE_REASONS) — no prose, no shell hazard — so only the
# two free-text resume-contract scalars are carried. `next_action` is the one that actually bit: it
# is a one-line concrete next step, which is exactly where paths and verb names (hence backticks)
# live.
PAUSE_STDIN_CONFLICTING_ARGV_FLAGS = (
    ("resume_from", "--resume-from", "resume_from", None),
    ("next_action", "--next-action", "next_action", None),
)
PROSE_BEARING_PAUSE_FIELDS = ("resume_from", "next_action")

# `task refuse`: the stdin mapping carries the free-text reason ALONE, so the table is one row. That
# reason is carried VERBATIM into the fleet-verdict basis, so a shell-eaten fragment there misroutes
# the controller's next decision rather than merely reading oddly.
REFUSE_STDIN_CONFLICTING_ARGV_FLAGS = (
    ("reason", "--reason", "reason", None),
)
