# ── T-11413 (X-1081 / X-1082) — THE AUTHORING VERB FOR `live_probe.settled_by` ────────────────────
# The field was READ three ways and WRITTEN none: the grammar half (`_live_probe_settled_grammar_error`),
# the resolution half (`_live_probe_settled_evidence_unresolved`) and the overdue-recheck lens
# (`views.py` via `_live_probe_waiver_settled`) all judge a value nothing could author. So the honest
# discharge SPEC-0094 §3 defines needed a hand-edit of governed YAML — which skips the emit — while the
# two exits the kernel itself calls dishonest (re-dating `recheck_by`, flipping `user_facing: false`)
# were the shorter path. The verb surface made the wrong answer easier than the right one.
#
# ONE FIELD, ONE GRAMMAR, TWO SEAMS — the shape `live_probe` already has, and the shape the ACCEPTANCE
# probes already have (`--probe` at close, `--settle-probe` late, T-11107):
#   * `task update --live-probe-settled-*` — the PRE-CLOSE leg, riding the EXISTING waiver leg of the
#     live-probe mode (T-11209). No new mode, and NO terminal-card carve-out: `task update` keeps
#     refusing every done/wont-do card exactly as it does today.
#   * `task close --settle-live-probe-*` — the DONE-CARD leg, a fourth mode on the late-linkage branch
#     beside --closes-fp / --pv-* / --settle-probe. This is where a settleable waiver actually LIVES:
#     a waiver is authored to satisfy the close-gate, i.e. AT close, so by construction it is on a card
#     already `done` (<project> X-1082 measured that on six cards).
# The done-card leg lands on `task close` rather than on `task update` for a load-bearing reason:
# `task update` is the verb BEFORE the gate (D-0033) — it writes DIRTY and `task close` folds the write
# into the closure commit. On a done card there is no close left to fold it, so the write would sit
# dirty on `main`, where `tasks/*.yaml` is in land's NON-bookkeeping set and wedges every later land in
# the repo (the X-0274 class T-11162 paid to fix). `cmd_task_close`'s done branch already OWNS that
# posture fork, and already has `_live_probe_settled_unresolved` — the close-gate's own resolver —
# wired into it, so the leg costs no new machinery, no new injection and no new event type.
_LIVE_PROBE_SETTLED_KEYS = card_io._LIVE_PROBE_SETTLED_KEYS  # re-export alias (T-12702): moved module-local into task_card_io.py


_parse_live_probe_settled_by = card_io._parse_live_probe_settled_by  # re-export alias (T-12702): moved to task_card_io.py


