def _spec_is_active(spec_id: str, *, spec_dirs) -> bool:
    """Is `spec_id` ACTIVE in the corpus this checkout reads? PURE-ish, read-only, never raises.

    WHY A STATUS READ IS THE GATE (T-11157). GRAPH §What-a-spec-is-FOR: "`active` is the ONLY normative
    spec status — a spec governs only when `active`; any other status is non-authoritative and cannot
    satisfy adoption/closure." So gating the exemption on SPEC-0178's own status is not a feature flag
    and not a new gate — it makes "no case may be live until the activating card lands" a property of
    the EXISTING spec-lifecycle mechanism, which is exactly why SPEC-0178's activation was moved onto
    its last card. This card ships the carrier INERT: SPEC-0178 stays `proposed`, so a project may
    declare a case and every matching card still takes audit-post (AC7 proves that differentially,
    rather than the plan asserting it).

    RESOLUTION ORDER — own repo FIRST, then the engine, deduped: the `_kernel_content_file` idiom
    (bin/yitc-v2), reused rather than reinvented. The engine leg is NOT optional polish — a CONSUMER
    carries no kernel spec of its own (SPEC-0092: a bare id resolves consumer-own, else kernel), and
    consumers are precisely who this mechanism is FOR (the kernel declares no ops contract and can
    host no case at all). Without it the exemption would be dead for its entire audience. Own-wins
    keeps a consumer that owns a spec at the same id reading ITS spec, unchanged.

    FAIL-CLOSED: an absent id, an unreadable or mis-shaped spec file, an unreadable directory, or no
    `spec_dirs` at all ⇒ False ⇒ audit-post is taken. Not-provably-active is treated as not-active."""
    seen: set = set()
    for d in (spec_dirs or ()):
        try:
            key = str(Path(d).resolve())
        except Exception:
            key = str(d)
        if key in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(key)
        try:
            for p in state.scan_specs(Path(d)):
                doc = state.load_path(p)
                if isinstance(doc, dict) and str(doc.get("id") or "").strip() == spec_id:
                    return str(doc.get("status") or "").strip() == "active"
        except Exception:
            continue                           # an unreadable corpus proves nothing — keep looking
    return False


def _card_ship_footprint(sha, *, commit_chain, task_diff_files, repo_root):
    """T-11404 (SPEC-0178 rule 3) — the card's SHIPPED FOOTPRINT: the paths its OWN ship commit(s)
    changed. Returns `(paths, unproven)` — the sorted path set, and the recorded ship shas whose diff
    could NOT be read. Read-only git. Every read this helper PERFORMS is total and cannot raise;
    the CALLER's injected floor reader is deliberately NOT wrapped and its failure propagates —
    the exact scope, and why, under "WHAT 'TOTAL, NEVER RAISES' COVERS" below.

    WHY THIS EXISTS — `_task_diff_files` answers a DIFFERENT question. That helper diffs
    `merge-base(<sha>, main)..<sha>`: "what does this branch ADD relative to CURRENT main". For rule 3
    the question is "what did this CARD SHIP", and the two come apart in two ordinary ways that
    COMPOUND: `worktree sync` merges `main` forward into the branch (a preamble instruction), and the
    D-0082 recorded commit is the LAST `commit_landed`, which a Stage-8 mode-a absorption cycle makes a
    bookkeeping commit rather than the ship. Once `main` has independently converged on the shipped
    content, the branch-vs-main TREE diff shows the deliverable as NOTHING and rule 3 reads a set that
    holds only the bookkeeping paths.

    MEASURED, not theorised (<project> T-0442, closure 2026-08-21T12:14Z — X-1062): recorded commit
    c13267a, `main` 6042a8ab, `git diff --name-only 6042a8ab..c13267a` = the audit YAML, the journal
    and the task card. The card's deliverable 0ba5ec21 changed `tests/test_alembic_single_head.py` —
    inside rule 3's named set — and the exemption survived. The guard was evaluated against the one
    diff that by construction never carries the work.

    THE SHIP RECORD IS THE JOURNAL'S, NOT A NEW STORE. `commit_chain` is `_task_commit_landed_chain`'s
    ordered `[(sha, kind|None)]` (T-10567), where `kind is None` is the already-documented SHIP
    discriminator — `cmd_task_commit` emits no kind, the bookkeeping self-commits (park / pause /
    wont-do) and the close-tail kinds do. So the footprint is DERIVED from a record `task commit`
    already writes: no new event, field, store or reader (CHARTER §P1 F2).

    THE EXISTING SET IS A FLOOR, NEVER A REPLACEMENT. `task_diff_files(sha)` is unioned in, so this can
    only ever ADD paths — the fail-closed direction rule 3 requires, and the reason it cannot
    over-revoke: the closure-record commit does not exist yet at this seam, so ordinary closing
    bookkeeping is not in either half (AC2).

    WHAT "TOTAL, NEVER RAISES" COVERS — the scope, stated because it is not the whole call
    (audit-post pass-1 + pass-2 MEDIUM: pass 2 asked for the unqualified claim above to be qualified
    at every occurrence, not only explained here). Every read this helper PERFORMS is guarded: the git-context probe and
    the per-commit `git show` cannot raise or exit nonzero out of here. The FLOOR read
    `task_diff_files(sha)` is the CALLER's injected reader, and its failure is deliberately NOT caught:
    a close that cannot read the card's changed paths at all must ABORT rather than proceed on a
    narrowed set, which is strictly more fail-closed than reporting it as `unproven` — and that
    propagation is the atomicity property `task close` already has (T-0638 injects a raising
    `_task_diff_files` to prove a crash before the done-write flips nothing). Swallowing it here would
    convert a hard abort into a soft one and weaken a guarantee this card has no business touching.

    UNREADABILITY IS REPORTED, NEVER DEGRADED (audit-pre pass-1 HIGH). A recorded ship sha that does
    not resolve, or whose diff `git show` refuses, is returned in `unproven` rather than silently
    contributing nothing — degrading it to the floor alone would preserve the exemption in exactly the
    case the footprint cannot be proven, which is this card's own failure direction. The CALLER decides
    what to do with it (`cmd_task_close` refuses the exemption and says why). An EMPTY read is NOT an
    unreadable one: a MERGE commit legitimately prints no name-only paths — which is also why a
    `worktree sync` merge contributes nothing here and the card is credited with its OWN work, never
    with main's.

    "UNPROVABLE" IS A CLAIM ABOUT A REPOSITORY THAT EXISTS. The unproven set is computed only when
    `repo_root` resolves as a git work tree; where it does not, git can answer nothing, so a read
    failure carries no information and the helper returns the floor with an EMPTY unproven set. This
    is not a hole in the fail-closed rule above: `task close` cannot reach this seam outside a real
    checkout — it has already resolved the recorded commit through `_git_resolve_sha` and read the
    branch-vs-main diff — so the only callers in that state are harnesses that stub both. Keeping the
    claim honest there is what stops a git-less sandbox from reading as a proven governance breach."""
    import subprocess

    def _git(*a):
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo_root), *a], capture_output=True, text=True,
                              check=False)

    try:
        _has_git = _git("rev-parse", "--git-dir").returncode == 0
    except Exception:
        _has_git = False
    # The FLOOR read is the CALLER's injected reader and its failure PROPAGATES — deliberately, see
    # the docstring's scope note. Everything this helper adds below is total.
    paths, unproven = set(task_diff_files(sha) or []), []
    for entry in (commit_chain or []):
        try:
            csha, kind = entry[0], entry[1]
        except Exception:
            continue
        if kind is not None:                   # bookkeeping self-commit / close-tail — not a ship
            continue
        csha = str(csha or "").strip()
        if not csha:
            continue
        try:
            r = _git("show", "--name-only", "--format=", csha)
        except Exception:
            if _has_git:
                unproven.append(csha)
            continue
        if r.returncode != 0:
            if _has_git:
                unproven.append(csha)
            continue
        paths.update(p for p in r.stdout.splitlines() if p.strip())
    return sorted(paths), sorted(set(unproven))


def _task_diff_files(sha: str, *, REPO_ROOT) -> list[str]:
    """The FULL task diff's touched paths (name-only) — used by the T-0202 hygiene-fast-path
    excluded-surface WARN in `task close`. Mirrors _get_audit_post_diff's range logic: a task may
    span >1 commit (T-0182), so base = `git merge-base <sha> main` and the range is `base..sha`;
    fallback (no `main` / merge-base fails / base == sha) = the single commit `git show <sha>`.
    Returns [] on any git failure (the caller's WARN is non-blocking — a diff-fetch miss must never
    break closure). `sha` is the recorded-commit SHA the caller already resolved (D-0082), not HEAD.
    T-11999 (X-1237) — every returned line is DECODED through the shared `textutil.git_unquote_path`
    (T-11772). Under git's default `core.quotepath` a `--name-only` listing prints a non-ASCII path
    escaped and quoted (`"design/\\320\\264.md"`), which matches no glob the callers test it against
    — so the excluded-surface WARN and `_all_touched_non_runtime_surface` were reading a transport
    form rather than the path git means. Identity on an unquoted path, so an ASCII-only listing is
    byte-identical."""
    import subprocess

    def _git(*args):
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(REPO_ROOT), *args],
                              capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)

    def _paths(out):
        # `textutil` is this module's own module-level import (top of file, beside `state`/`events`)
        # — named here because a diff-only reader of THIS hunk cannot see it and reads the call as an
        # undefined name (the pass-1 audit-post finding on T-11999 did exactly that).
        return [q for q in (textutil.git_unquote_path(x).strip() for x in out.splitlines()) if q]

    base = _git("merge-base", sha, "main")
    base_sha = base.stdout.strip() if base.returncode == 0 else ""
    head_sha = _git("rev-parse", sha).stdout.strip()
    if base_sha and base_sha != head_sha:
        r = _git("diff", "--name-only", f"{base_sha}..{sha}")
        if r.returncode == 0:
            return _paths(r.stdout)
    r = _git("show", "--name-only", "--format=", sha)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        return []
    return _paths(r.stdout)

def _task_diff_range(sha: str, *, REPO_ROOT) -> "tuple[str | None, str]":
    """The (base, head) revs of a task's cumulative diff (T-0616) — the range `_task_diff_files`
    derives its touched set from, exposed so the symbol-level drift scoping (`_symbol_changed_in_range`)
    compares the SAME range. head = `git rev-parse <sha>`; base = `git merge-base <sha> main` when that
    is present and != head (the multi-commit task range, T-0182), else the single-commit fallback
    base = `<sha>^` (the commit's first parent) — mirroring `_task_diff_files`'s `git show <sha>`
    fallback. base is None when no parent exists (root commit) / git fails: the caller then passes no
    range and the drift check stays file-level (back-compat). Read-only git; never raises."""
    import subprocess

    def _git(*args):
        return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(REPO_ROOT), *args],
                              capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)

    head_sha = _git("rev-parse", sha).stdout.strip()
    base = _git("merge-base", sha, "main")
    base_sha = base.stdout.strip() if base.returncode == 0 else ""
    if base_sha and base_sha != head_sha:
        return base_sha, head_sha
    parent = _git("rev-parse", f"{sha}^").stdout.strip()
    return (parent or None), head_sha

def _dangling_binding_warn_text(tid: str, dangling: list, *, check_failed: bool,
                                is_consumer: bool, commit_route: str) -> str:
    """The T-0455 close-time binding WARN text (T-12978). The ENGINE text promises a land `graph
    conformance` ABORT — true there, because the kernel's own tests/ suite runs conformance at land
    verify. A `-C` CONSUMER land runs the consumer's declared verify layers and NEVER runs binding
    conformance (proven by tests/test_close_warn_consumer_land_truth.py), so on a consumer the WARN
    states that instead of promising an ABORT that does not happen. Engine text is unchanged."""
    if check_failed:
        if is_consumer:
            return (f"WARN: {tid} — the dangling-activated-spec check could not run (spec corpus load "
                    "failed); the close-time binding check was SKIPPED, and binding conformance is not "
                    "land-verified in this project, so nothing later will flag it. Verify each activated "
                    "spec declares a valid `binding:` token now (T-0455). "
                    f"{commit_route} Closure PROCEEDS (non-blocking visibility).\n")
        return (f"WARN: {tid} — the dangling-activated-spec check could not run (spec corpus load failed); "
                "the close-time binding check was SKIPPED, so a land `graph conformance` ABORT is still "
                "possible. Verify each activated spec declares a valid `binding:` token before `land` "
                f"(T-0455). {commit_route} Closure PROCEEDS (non-blocking visibility).\n")
    remedy = "\n".join(
        f"  - {sid}: `yitc-v2 spec edit {sid}` (declare/refresh its `binding:` token)"
        for sid in dangling)
    if is_consumer:
        return (f"WARN: {tid} activated spec(s) {', '.join(dangling)} with no valid `binding:` token "
                "(dangling/unknown-bound per `graph conformance`) — binding conformance is not "
                "land-verified in this project, so `land` will NOT catch it: the binding is yours to "
                "declare now:\n"
                f"{remedy}\n"
                "Declare/refresh each spec's binding BEFORE `land` (SPEC-0005 §7: the card that authors a spec "
                "declares its binding on the proposed head before audit-post; this post-close `spec edit` "
                "is the ACCEPTED recovery for a missed declaration — not the ordering, and not a defect). "
                f"{commit_route} Closure PROCEEDS "
                "(non-blocking visibility; prevention by discipline, detection by report — NOT a gate).\n")
    return (f"WARN: {tid} activated spec(s) {', '.join(dangling)} that the land "
            "conformance check would flag as dangling/unknown-bound (no valid `binding:` token) — "
            "a land `graph conformance` ABORT BEFORE you reach main, far from the cause (FP5/T-0437):\n"
            f"{remedy}\n"
            "Declare/refresh each spec's binding BEFORE `land` (SPEC-0005 §7: the card that authors a spec "
            "declares its binding on the proposed head before audit-post; this post-close `spec edit` "
            "is the ACCEPTED recovery for a missed declaration — not the ordering, and not a defect). "
            f"{commit_route} Closure PROCEEDS "
            "(non-blocking visibility; prevention by discipline, detection by report — NOT a gate).\n")


def _dangling_activated_specs(activated: list, *, SPECS_DIR, _build_binding_views) -> dict:
    """T-0455 (FP5) — of the spec ids a closing task JUST activated, which would the LAND
    conformance predicate flag as dangling (active + empty binding) or unknown-token-bound?

    PURE-ish f(activated) → {"dangling": [sorted ids], "derivation_failed": bool}. It re-uses the
    SINGLE canonical land-conformance derivation `_build_binding_views` (NO new store/detector — the
    EXACT predicate `graph conformance` / `land verify` runs, so the close-time WARN and the land
    flag never disagree, P5). It does NOT mutate, does NOT gate, and NEVER raises — a corpus read
    failure sets `derivation_failed: True` (a DISTINCT, honest signal — the check could not run; it
    must NOT silently degrade to an empty `dangling` list, which would read as "all clear" and hide
    the very FP5 state this exists to surface). Reads the on-disk spec set fresh because the
    just-flipped `proposed → active` write has already happened on disk when this runs (after
    `_activate_task_proposed_specs`).

    SPEC-0005 §7 / GRAPH §"NOT a validation layer" / CHARTER non-goal #7: this is detection-by-report
    moved to the closest non-gate point (close-time), NOT a code gate — the binding token is a
    SEMANTIC authoring choice the close path cannot synthesize. The caller emits an ACTIONABLE
    non-blocking WARN; closure PROCEEDS regardless."""
    ids = [s for s in (activated or []) if s]
    if not ids:
        return {"dangling": [], "derivation_failed": False}
    try:
        import yaml
        if not SPECS_DIR.is_dir():
            return {"dangling": [], "derivation_failed": True}
        specs = {}
        for p in state.scan_specs(SPECS_DIR):   # SPEC-* glob excludes _template.yaml
            d = state.load_str(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8")) or {}
            sid = d.get("id")
            if sid:
                specs[sid] = d
        _, _, report = _build_binding_views(specs)
        flagged = set(report.get("dangling_specs") or [])
        flagged |= {u.get("spec") for u in (report.get("unknown_tokens") or []) if u.get("spec")}
        return {"dangling": sorted(set(ids) & flagged), "derivation_failed": False}
    except Exception:
        # NEVER crash the close verb; never silently []-degrade — surface the inability honestly.
        return {"dangling": [], "derivation_failed": True}

_post_action_hint = closure_evidence._post_action_hint  # re-export alias (T-12697)

_batch_sibling_task_ids = closure_evidence._batch_sibling_task_ids  # re-export alias (T-12697)


_requires_ordered = closure_evidence._requires_ordered  # re-export alias (T-12697)


@functools.wraps(closure_evidence._declared_surface_preview)
def _declared_surface_preview(*args, **kw):
    kw = {**{"_requires_ordered": _requires_ordered}, **kw}
    return closure_evidence._declared_surface_preview(*args, **kw)

def _activate_task_proposed_specs(tid: str, *, SPECS_DIR, _append_event, _decision_apply_field_edits, _die, _find_spec_path, _read_yaml) -> tuple:
    """T-0199 (Part C) + T-0239 — at task closure, flip every PROPOSED spec the closing task
    ACTIVATES → status: active (sorted-id order), AND atomically flip each activated spec's
    `supersedes:` target (D-0047: an active target → superseded; a still-proposed target → withdrawn).
    Returns (activated_ids, superseded_ids).

    Activation ownership is read from the EXPLICIT carrier (SPEC-0005 §5 — the single authority,
    clarified by T-0238; NEVER derived from `cites`, which is informational only). The two explicit
    reads are ORDERED — EXPLICIT-CARRIER-WINS (T-10655), not a disjunction (one helper, no parallel):

    - **explicit owner (SPEC-0005 §5 / T-0238) — AUTHORITATIVE when present:** S names its activation
      owner via `activation_owner_task`; S activates at THIS closure IFF `activation_owner_task == tid`.
      For a plan-born spec this is the ONE decomposed task that `plan accept`'s decomposition chose to
      activate it; `proposed_by` stays the PLAN slug (provenance/corpus — the plan's proposed_by-keyed
      corpus + finalization gate keep seeing it, P5) and is NOT the activation owner.
    - **work-first (T-0199) — the fallback when NO owner is named:** S was born by this task and names
      no explicit owner, so `proposed_by == tid` owns it.

    When an owner IS named, `proposed_by` is PROVENANCE and is NOT consulted — provenance must never act
    as authority. Reading the two as a DISJUNCTION (`pb == tid or aot == tid`) let a FOREIGN task flip a
    spec it does not own: SPEC-0162 (`proposed_by: T-10612`, `activation_owner_task: T-10622`) was
    activated by T-10612's close (the 2026-07-17 incident, deviation
    `task-close-activates-spec-with-foreign-activation-owner-t10612-spec0162`; hand-restored by ad96b9f),
    violating the GRAPH §Spec-lifecycle sole-writer rule. T-10655 ordered the reads to close it.

    The retired derivation (T-0211 'the task cites BOTH the plan and the spec' → activating task) is
    DROPPED: an informational cites-both mis-activated a pilot spec (the T-0237 incident), proving the
    derived read unsafe — activation is carried explicitly now, no hidden carrier. (T-10655 is the SAME
    foreign-flip shape, closed for the remaining `proposed_by` arm.)

    MIRROR of `_birth_plan_draft_specs`: same comment-preserving `_decision_apply_field_edits`, NO
    parallel edit path, IDEMPOTENT (the status==proposed filter skips already-active specs). T-0638 —
    `cmd_task_close` now calls this AFTER the durable status:done write (atomic-closure ordering), so a
    crash before the done-write never reaches here (no spec flipped); a crash BETWEEN the done-write and
    here, or part-way through this loop, leaves the task `done` with its spec(s) un/partly-activated and
    is healed by `_recover_close_tail` re-running this idempotently. The per-spec writes flip the
    `supersedes:` TARGET FIRST, then the spec → active, so a resume COMPLETES the supersession instead of
    stranding a target un-flipped (the owned spec staying `proposed` is the resume re-entry signal).

    Lineage guard (audit-pre F1, D-0047 'one active lineage per spec'): the activation set MUST NOT
    activate two specs that supersede the SAME target. Validated BEFORE any mutation — fail-closed.

    T-10543 (X-0411) — each completed flip APPENDS a `spec_activated` event (spec_id + the activating
    task in the task_id slot + the supersedes flip it performed). Before this the sole `proposed→active`
    writer was SILENT: no activat* event existed in any journal, so the GRAPH drift-#10 check (an active
    spec with no activating carrier) had no evidence surface and a work-first spec-activating task could
    never satisfy P8 from its own activation (the recurring E-0005 WARN); `bin/lib/views.py` already
    NAMED `spec_activated` as a SUBSTANTIVE type that nothing emitted. The emit lives HERE, inside the
    one write, so BOTH call sites are covered from one home (P5) — `cmd_task_close` AND the
    `_recover_close_tail` crash-heal re-run, which is exactly where a caller-side emit would have gone
    missing. It is IDEMPOTENT for free: the `status == "proposed"` filter above skips an already-active
    spec, so the heal re-run re-emits nothing. Emitted AFTER the durable write it attests (mirroring the
    T-0638 target-first ordering) — a crash mid-loop leaves the spec `proposed`, so no event ever
    attests a flip that did not land."""
    owned = []  # (path, spec_id, supersedes_target)
    for p in state.scan_specs(SPECS_DIR):   # SPEC-* glob excludes _template.yaml
        d = _read_yaml(p)
        if not isinstance(d, dict):
            continue
        if d.get("status") != "proposed":
            continue
        sid = d.get("id")
        # EXPLICIT ownership only (SPEC-0005 §5) — never derived from cites — and EXPLICIT-CARRIER-WINS
        # (T-10655). The predicate itself lives in ONE pure home (T-11014) so this WRITER and the
        # read-only eligibility PREDICATE the activation-gated overlay consults cannot drift (P5).
        if _task_owns_proposed_spec(d, tid):
            owned.append((p, sid, d.get("supersedes")))
    # lineage guard — no two activated specs may supersede the same target (D-0047 one-active-lineage)
    seen_targets: dict = {}
    for _p, sid, tgt in owned:
        if tgt:
            if tgt in seen_targets:
                _die(f"{tid} activation set would activate two specs ({seen_targets[tgt]} + {sid}) "
                     f"that both supersede {tgt} — D-0047 one-active-lineage violation. Split the close "
                     "(one activating task per lineage) or chain them via requires/supersedes.")
            seen_targets[tgt] = sid
    # mutate: each owned proposed spec — flip its supersedes TARGET FIRST, then the spec itself active
    # (T-0638 — target-first so a crash between the two writes leaves the owned spec still `proposed`,
    # the resume re-entry signal; the re-run re-enters via the status==proposed scan and the target's
    # cur-status guard skips the already-flipped target → no orphaned supersession). The atomic
    # supersession of the target happens in the SAME close as the activation (D-0047 one-active-lineage).
    activated, superseded = [], []
    for p, sid, tgt in owned:
        flipped_target = None   # T-10543: report the flip that ACTUALLY happened, not a re-derived guess
        if tgt:
            tgt_path = _find_spec_path(tgt)
            if tgt_path is not None:
                cur = (_read_yaml(tgt_path) or {}).get("status")
                new = "superseded" if cur == "active" else ("withdrawn" if cur == "proposed" else None)
                if new:
                    traw = tgt_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
                    _decision_apply_field_edits(tgt_path, traw,
                                                [("status", f"status: {new}")], {"status": new})
                    superseded.append(tgt)
                    flipped_target = (tgt, new)
        raw = p.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        _decision_apply_field_edits(p, raw, [("status", "status: active")], {"status": "active"})
        if sid:
            activated.append(sid)
            # T-10543 (X-0411) — attest the completed flip on the ONE journal (see the docstring: one
            # emit site covers both call paths; the status==proposed filter keeps the heal re-run silent).
            data = {"spec_id": sid}
            if flipped_target:
                data["supersedes"], data["supersedes_status"] = flipped_target
            _append_event("spec_activated", tid, data)
    return sorted(activated), sorted(superseded)

